\section{Details about control variables}
\label{sec:control_variables}

I chose control variables in two steps.
First, I made a list of candidate variables, both from theory and from automated searches through variables in the WDI.
Then, I excluded candidates for two reasons.
First, I excluded variables that represent treatments, because treatments mediate the marginal-cost relationship between life expectancy and the price of life.
This includes variables such as healthcare spending, nutrition, and water quality/sanitation.
I discuss why this is necessary in more detail above.
Second, I excluded variables for which I had no reason to expect a relationship with the cost-effectiveness of treatments.
I list the remaining variables, with my reason for including each, in \autoref{tab:control_variables_table}.

I logged a few variables that are right skewed and represent positive (or almost always positive) quantities.
I list these variables in \autoref{tab:logged_variables_table}.

%% begin.rcode logged_variables_table, echo = FALSE
% kable(
%   logged_variable_table,
%   caption = "Logged variables"
% ) %>%
%   bold_header()
%% end.rcode

Then, I removed a few variables to create an estimable model.
Unfortunately, the model with all control variables was unestimable.
This is because each additional control variable not only added a degree of freedom to the model, but also necessitated excluding more rows due to missing data.
So, I chose controls to prioritize explaining the most variation, by iteratively adding controls to maximally increase the $R^2$ of the second-stage regression.
I was able to create an estimable model with all but a few of the variables.
In the \autoref{tab:control_variables_table}, I list variables I excluded for estimability as ``unestimable''.

Next, I removed several variables due to multicollinearity.
To check for robustness, I report the results with all estimable control variables in \autoref{sec:specification_appendix}.
With all these variables, the modeled life expectancy coefficient is not statistically significant ($p = \rinline{multicollinear_p_value}$).
However, many of these control variables are highly correlated with modeled life expectancy, leading to imprecise coefficient estimates.
To address this problem, I iteratively excluded the control variable with the highest second-stage $GVIF^{\frac{1}{2 * df}}$ until the $GVIF^{\frac{1}{2 * df}}$ values for all control variables and modeled life expectancy were below $\sqrt{\rinline{vif_cutoff^2}}$.
This is a relatively conservative cut-off.
In the \autoref{tab:control_variables_table}, I list variables I excluded due to multicollinearity as ``multicollinear''.
Unfortunately, this leaves open the unlikely possibility that the effect I observed was not due to modeled life expectancy, but one of the many variables collinear with modeled life expectancy instead.

Although several control variables were statistically insignificant in the main regression, I still used these variables as controls for the main regression.
However, for the model I used to predict the marginal cost of life, I excluded insignificant controls to prevent overfitting.
To do so, I iteratively removed the most statistically insignificant control until all control coefficients were statistically significant at the 5\% level.
In the end, no controls were significant, so the prediction model includes only life expectancy and year fixed effects.
In the \autoref{tab:control_variables_table}, I list the controls I removed for the prediction model as ``insignificant''.

%% begin.rcode control_variables_table, echo = FALSE
% kable(
%   controls_table,
%   caption = "Control variables, with my reason for including each"
% ) %>%
%   column_spec(1, width = "3.2cm") %>%
%   column_spec(3, width = "5.5cm") %>%
%   bold_header(font_size = 9)
%% end.rcode

\section{Regression statistics}
\label{sec:regression_statistics}

In \autoref{tab:regression_statistics}, I report statistics from three different models: the base model, the model including multi-collinear controls, and the model excluding insignificant variables.

%% begin.rcode regression_statistics, echo = FALSE
% kable(
%   regression_statistics_table |> 
%     mutate_all(linebreak),
%   caption = "Statistics from three specifications: the base model, the model including multi-collinear controls, and the model excluding insignificant variables",
%   digits = 64,
%   escape = FALSE
% ) %>%
%   bold_header()
%% end.rcode

I interpret these statistics as follows.
From the low value of the first-stage F-test p-value, I conclude that the variables I used to predict life expectancy were jointly significant.
From the low value of the Wu-Hausman test, I conclude that there is true endogeneity.
More specifically, I can conclude that the marginal benefit of life curve must have a non-zero slope.

\section{Hausman test}
\label{sec:hausman_appendix}

I conducted a Hausman test to check for inconsistency between the random effects model and the fixed effects model.
The Hausman test had a p-value of \rinline{signif(unique_hausman_p_value, display_figures)}.
Thus, there is no evidence that the fixed effects model and the random effects model are inconsistent.
However, because the fixed effects estimates are very imprecise, this test has little power, so this is only weak evidence that the random effects model is consistent.

\section{Stationarity}
\label{sec:stationarity_tests}

I tested the residuals from the random effects model for stationarity within each country.
This is because non-stationarity could result in invalid estimates.
For the tests to work, I could only include countries with at least 
\rinline{minimum_years}\
years of data.
I considered only a lag of one, and did not allow for different intercepts between countries or consistent time trends within countries.
I report these results in \autoref{tab:stationarity_tests_table}.
From the low p-value for all tests, I conclude that the residuals are stationary.
However, with as few as \rinline{minimum_years} years of data for some countries, these tests have limited power.

%% begin.rcode stationarity_tests_table, echo = FALSE
% kable(stationarity_table, caption = "Stationarity test results") %>%
%   bold_header()
%% end.rcode

\section{Price robustness tests}
\label{sec:prices_appendix}

Here, I consider compare the results for alternative sets of prices.
I report the results in \autoref{fig:prices_robustness_figure}.

%% begin.rcode prices_robustness_figure, echo = FALSE, fig.cap = "95\\% confidence intervals for the life expectancy coefficient with different prices"
% plot_confidence_intervals(prices_robustness_table, "Prices")
%% end.rcode

I report my main result using ``article prices''.

For each study, Tufts reports both the published price and an alternative price calculated by a reader.
I choose to use the published prices because these prices came from peer-reviewed papers.
However, for the ``with reader adjustments'' result, I used the figures from the readers instead.

Tufts reports whether prices were in units of currency per QALY gained or currency per DALY avoided.
A DALY is a disability-adjusted life-year, and represents a year of life with significant disability.
For the main result, I used only QALY prices.
However, for the ``with DALY prices'' result, I used both kinds of figures.

I interpret these results as follows.
Including both QALY and DALY ratios results in a slightly higher estimate.
This suggests slight differences between the two methods of calculation.
However, using the reader-adjusted figures results in a much wider confidence interval with a lower central estimate.
This suggests that readers are adjusting figures in such a way as to reduce variation between countries and years.
The central estimate using reader-adjusted prices is still practically high, and the confidence interval fully overlaps my main estimate.
Thus, I am not overly worried about this discrepancy.

Funders might also influence which treatments researchers study.
Foundations, nonprofits, and intergovernmental organizations might fund studies of particularly cost-effective treatments in developing countries, regardless of which treatments those countries actually pay for.
Likewise, the pharmaceutical and medical device industry might fund studies of particularly expensive treatments in developed countries.
Either kind of selection could exaggerate the differences in prices between countries.
To check for such selection, I repeated my calculations excluding studies with each kind of funding.
I report the results in \autoref{fig:funding_robustness_figure}.
Excluding these studies has little effect on the estimate, so selection by funders does not seem to drive my results.

%% begin.rcode funding_robustness_figure, echo = FALSE, fig.cap = "95\\% confidence intervals for the life expectancy coefficient excluding studies by funding source"
% plot_confidence_intervals(funding_robustness_table, "Studies")
%% end.rcode

\section{Composition of treatments}
\label{sec:composition_appendix}

In my model, moving along the marginal cost of life curve means shifting from more to less cost-effective treatments.
If this shift happens between broad categories of treatments, then studies in countries with higher life expectancy should more often be of categories of treatments with high prices.

To test this, I split the life expectancy coefficient into two parts.
First, for each category of treatments, I calculated the average log price across all countries and years.
Then, for each country-year, I calculated a composition index, the average of the category averages for the studies in that country-year, and a within-category price, the average difference between each log price and its category average.
The log price of life in a country-year is the sum of these two parts.
So, using the same regression as for my main estimate, the coefficients for the two parts add up to the life expectancy coefficient.

I used two kinds of categories from the CEA registry: intervention types (for example, pharmaceutical, screening, or immunization), and disease categories from the Global Burden of Disease.
The registry reports disease categories for only \rinline{disease_country_years} country-years, so I estimated the total coefficient separately for these country-years.
I report the results in \autoref{tab:composition_table}.

%% begin.rcode composition_table, echo = FALSE
% kable(
%   composition_table |>
%     mutate_all(linebreak),
%   caption = "The life expectancy coefficient, split into composition and within-category parts, with 95\\% confidence intervals in parentheses. $N$ is the number of country-years.",
%   escape = FALSE
% ) %>%
%   column_spec(1, width = "2.5cm") %>%
%   bold_header(font_size = 8)
%% end.rcode

Across intervention types, composition explains almost none of the coefficient.
Also, the share of studies of pharmaceuticals does not increase with life expectancy: the coefficient is
\rinline{pharmaceutical_share_interval$`50%`}
(95\% confidence interval
\rinline{pharmaceutical_share_interval$`2.5%`} to
\rinline{pharmaceutical_share_interval$`97.5%`}).
Thus, I find no evidence that countries with higher life expectancy shift between broad categories of treatments.
With finer categories, composition explains a larger share of the coefficient, but these estimates are imprecise and based on fewer country-years.

There are two possible explanations.
First, the shift might happen within the broad categories in the registry: for example, from screening high-risk patients to screening low-risk patients, or from generic drugs to newer brand-name drugs.
Second, the same treatments might be more expensive per QALY in countries with higher life expectancy, for reasons outside my model.
My data cannot distinguish between these explanations.

\section{Comparison with published estimates}
\label{sec:validation_appendix}

Ochalek et al. \autocite*{Ochalek_2018} estimated the cost per DALY averted by health spending in \rinline{nrow(ochalek_raw_estimates)} low- and middle-income countries in \rinline{ochalek_year}, using four methods.
Rather than using prices from cost-effectiveness studies, they estimated the effect of health spending on mortality and morbidity across countries.
So, their estimates provide an independent check on my predictions.
They report costs in \rinline{ochalek_year} US dollars, so I converted them to \rinline{deflator_year} international dollars using the same exchange rates and deflators I used for prices.
DALYs and QALYs are not identical, but both measure years of healthy life, so the two sets of estimates should be roughly comparable.

In \autoref{fig:ochalek_figure}, I compare my predictions for the \rinline{nrow(ochalek_comparison)} countries in both datasets to the geometric mean of their four estimates.
The correlation between the logs of the two is \rinline{signif(ochalek_correlation, 2)}.
On average, my predictions are \rinline{signif(ochalek_ratio, 2)} times theirs.
For \rinline{ochalek_overlaps} of these countries, the range of their four estimates overlaps the 95\% confidence interval for my prediction.

%% begin.rcode ochalek_figure, echo = FALSE, fig.asp = 1, fig.cap = paste0("My predicted marginal cost of life compared to the cost per DALY averted estimated by Ochalek et al., in ", ochalek_year, ". Vertical bars are 95\\% confidence intervals for my predictions, horizontal bars are the range of their four estimates, and the dashed line marks equal values.")
% ochalek_figure
%% end.rcode

The largest disagreements are for \rinline{knitr::combine_words(ochalek_disagreements)}, where my predictions are far lower than theirs.
All of these countries are in southern Africa, and have very high rates of HIV/AIDS, so their life expectancy is far lower than their income would suggest.
This supports my caution above: a single marginal cost of life curve may exaggerate differences in the marginal cost of life between countries with unusual disease burdens.

To check whether HIV explains these disagreements, I added baseline HIV prevalence (see \autoref{sec:specification_appendix}) to my prediction model.
With this control, the correlation between the logs of my predictions and their estimates rises from \rinline{signif(ochalek_correlation, 2)} to \rinline{signif(hiv_ochalek_correlation, 2)}, and my predictions for these countries move much closer to theirs; see \autoref{tab:hiv_disagreements_table}.

%% begin.rcode hiv_disagreements_table, echo = FALSE
% kable(
%   hiv_disagreements_table,
%   caption = paste0("The countries where my predictions disagree most with the estimates of Ochalek et al., in ", deflator_year, " international \\$ per QALY or DALY, with and without controlling for baseline HIV prevalence")
% ) %>%
%   column_spec(2:4, width = "2.6cm") %>%
%   bold_header(font_size = 9)
%% end.rcode

\section{Specification robustness tests}
\label{sec:specification_appendix}

There were a number of decisions I had to make in order choose a model specification.

First, I had to decide how to account for country-level variation.
Ideally, I would use fixed effects by country.
However, the resulting confidence interval for the life expectancy coefficient was very wide and included zero; see \autoref{fig:specification_robustness_figure}.
This suggests there was not enough variation within countries in order to get a precise estimate without considering variation between countries.
Alternatively, I could use random effects or a pooled model.
The only advantage of using random effects is that a random effects model could result in more precise estimates.
In this case, using random effects resulted in less precise estimates; see \autoref{fig:filtered_specification_robustness_figure}.
Thus, for simplicity, I choose to use the pooled model.

I also had to decide what to do about variation in price of life observations within a country-year.
Some country-years had as many as \rinline{max(filter_price_data(summarized_prices)$number_of_observations)} price of life observations, and others had none at all.
For the main model, as I discussed above, I simply averaged the log price of life observations within a country-year.
Alternately, to correctly take into account variation within a country-year, I created a hierarchical Bayesian model.
I specified this model as follows.
I standardized all regressors.
Instead of using modeled life expectancy in the second stage, I used observed life expectancy, and modeled the residuals as correlated between the two stages.
I used dummy variables for year fixed effects.
For each stage, I estimated random effects by country with non-centered parameterization, allowing correlation in the random effects between the two stages.
Finally, I modeled random variation around a true mean for log price of life observations.
I used standard normal priors for coefficients, exponential (1) priors for standard deviations, and LKJ (2) priors for correlation matrices.
As the central estimate from the Bayesian model was comparable to the estimate from the pooled model, I decided to use the pooled model for the main result for simplicity.

I also considered alternate sets of control variables.
For the ``with multicollinear controls'' model, I included the control variables that I excluded due to multicollinearity.
This results in a wider confidence interval; see \autoref{fig:filtered_specification_robustness_figure}.
For the ``significant'' controls, I only included statistically significant controls.
This results in a narrower confidence interval; see \autoref{fig:filtered_specification_robustness_figure}.
Neither set yields a significantly different central estimate, suggesting that the estimate is not sensitive to which of these controls I include.
However, this cannot rule out confounding by variables I did not observe.

Finally, I considered ignoring endogeneity by using observed life expectancy directly as a regressor.
To do so means assuming that the slope of the marginal benefit of life curve is zero.
This results in a lower, but still statistically and practically significant, estimate; see \autoref{fig:filtered_specification_robustness_figure}.
This suggests that the slope of the marginal benefit of curve is negative, but not negative enough to outweigh the strongly positive slope of the marginal cost curve.

In the main model, I weighted all country-years equally, because my model describes country-years, not individual studies.
However, some country-years have many more studies than others, so their average prices should be more precise.
For the ``weighted by number of studies'' model, I weighted each country-year by its number of studies.
This results in a higher central estimate with a wider confidence interval; see \autoref{fig:filtered_specification_robustness_figure}.

For the ``unadjusted life expectancy'' model, I used unadjusted life expectancy from the World Bank instead of health-adjusted life expectancy.
This results in a slightly lower central estimate; see \autoref{fig:filtered_specification_robustness_figure}.
This is expected, because in my sample, one more year of unadjusted life expectancy corresponds to only about \rinline{health_adjustment_slope} more years of health-adjusted life expectancy.

I also considered two controls for differences in disease burden between countries.
For the ``with Sub-Saharan Africa dummy'' model, I included a dummy variable for countries in Sub-Saharan Africa.
For the ``with baseline HIV prevalence'' model, I included the prevalence of HIV among adults aged 15 to 49 in \rinline{hiv_baseline_year}, before antiretroviral treatment became widely available in low- and middle-income countries.
The World Bank does not report HIV prevalence in \rinline{hiv_baseline_year} for some countries with low prevalence, so I set HIV prevalence to zero for \rinline{knitr::combine_words(missing_HIV_countries)}.
Both models result in higher central estimates, with wider confidence intervals; see \autoref{fig:filtered_specification_robustness_figure}.
I did not include these controls in my main model for two reasons.
First, neither control is statistically significant ($p = \rinline{disease_control_p_values[1]}$ and $p = \rinline{disease_control_p_values[2]}$, respectively).
Second, HIV prevalence partly reflects how much countries spent on prevention, which is itself a movement along the marginal cost of life curve.
However, within Sub-Saharan Africa, HIV prevalence in \rinline{hiv_baseline_year} was, if anything, higher in richer countries (the correlation between HIV prevalence and log GDP per capita was \rinline{signif(african_hiv_income_correlation, 2)}), so differences in HIV prevalence there mostly reflect differences in the epidemic rather than differences in ability to pay.

To check for reverse causality from health to GDP per capita, I also used GDP per capita from previous years as an instrument.
Because PPP exchange rates start in \rinline{min(gdp_by_year$year)}, the longest lag excludes some early country-years.
Using GDP per capita from previous years yields similar or slightly higher estimates; see \autoref{fig:lagged_gdp_figure}.

%% begin.rcode lagged_gdp_figure, echo = FALSE, fig.cap = "95\\% confidence intervals for the life expectancy coefficient using GDP per capita from previous years as the instrument"
% plot_confidence_intervals(lagged_gdp_table, "GDP lag (years)")
%% end.rcode

I clustered standard errors for all models except for the hierarchical Bayesian model by country.
Thus, standard errors should be robust to heteroskedasticity and within-country autocorrelation.

The results for all the alternatives are in \autoref{fig:specification_robustness_figure}.

%% begin.rcode specification_robustness_figure, echo = FALSE, fig.cap = "95\\% confidence intervals for the life expectancy coefficient with different model specifications"
% plot_confidence_intervals(specification_robustness_table, "Specification")
%% end.rcode

For visual clarity, in \autoref{fig:filtered_specification_robustness_figure}, I exclude the fixed-effects specification.

%% begin.rcode filtered_specification_robustness_figure, echo = FALSE, fig.cap = "95\\% confidence intervals for the life expectancy coefficient with different model specifications, excluding the fixed effects model"
% specification_robustness_table |>
%   filter(Specification != "fixed effects") |>
%   plot_confidence_intervals("Specification")
%% end.rcode

\section{Price adjustments robustness tests}
\label{sec:price_adjustments_appendix}

To compare prices between different countries, I used PPPs for GDP, and to deflate prices, I used the GDP deflators.
I did this for the following reason.
Ideally, I could adjust prices from different countries and years, such that, for the same price in all countries and years, one could buy the same treatments.
Treatments include goods like healthcare, nutrition, and water quality/saniation.
Some treatments are traded internationally (like food) while others (like water sanitation) are not.
Also, some treatments are part of private consumption (like food) but others are not (like public water sanitation).
Finally, some treatments are proper healthcare goods, while others are not.
So, I choose to use price adjustments based on GDP goods as whole.
This excludes nominal exchange rates (which are based only on internationally traded goods) and CPIs (which are based only on private consumption goods).
However, for robustness, I considered alternative combinations of exchange rates and deflators.

In \autoref{fig:gdp_deflators_robustness_figure}, I compare the results from using different sets of  deflators to adjust GDP.

%% begin.rcode gdp_deflators_robustness_figure, echo = FALSE, fig.cap = "95\\% confidence intervals for the life expectancy coefficient with different GDP deflators"
% plot_confidence_intervals(gdp_deflators_robustness_table, "GDP deflators")
%% end.rcode

In \autoref{fig:gdp_exchange_robustness_figure}, I compare the results from using different sets of exchange rates to adjust GDP.

%% begin.rcode gdp_exchange_robustness_figure, echo = FALSE, fig.cap = "95\\% confidence intervals for the life expectancy coefficient with different GDP exchange rates"
% plot_confidence_intervals(gdp_exchange_robustness_table, "GDP exchange rates")
%% end.rcode

In \autoref{fig:price_deflators_figure}, I compare the results from using different sets of deflators to adjust prices.

%% begin.rcode price_deflators_figure, echo = FALSE, fig.cap = "95\\% confidence intervals for the life expectancy coefficient with different price deflators"
% plot_confidence_intervals(price_deflators_table, "Price deflators")
%% end.rcode

In \autoref{fig:price_exchange_figure}, I compare the results from using different sets of  exchange rates to adjust prices.

%% begin.rcode price_exchange_figure, echo = FALSE, fig.cap = "95\\% confidence intervals for the life expectancy coefficient with different price exchange rates"
% plot_confidence_intervals(price_exchange_table, "Price exchange rates")
%% end.rcode

I interpret these results as follows.
The choice of deflator has very little effect, likely due to year fixed-effects absorbing deflator differences.
The choice of exchange rate has minor effects, with one exception.
The International Comparison Program (ICP) reports PPP exchange rates for health goods only in benchmark years (2005, 2011, 2017, and 2021).
Using only these years leaves \rinline{benchmark_health_ppp_observations} country-years, and with GDP converted using health PPPs, GDP per capita becomes a weak instrument (first-stage $F$ of \rinline{benchmark_health_ppp_first_stage_f}), so the resulting estimate is unreliable.
On the same country-years, using PPPs for GDP yields an estimate similar to my main estimate, so the difference is not due to the subsample.
To use health PPPs for all years, I interpolated the relative price of health (the ratio of health PPPs to GDP PPPs) linearly between benchmark years, holding it constant before the first and after the last benchmark year.
Using these interpolated health PPPs to convert either GDP or prices yields central estimates very close to my main estimate; see \autoref{fig:gdp_exchange_robustness_figure} and \autoref{fig:price_exchange_figure}.
Thus, my results are not driven by health goods being relatively more expensive in richer countries.

\section{Two-stage least squares equations}
\label{sec:two_stage_least_squares_equations}

Here, I derive the equations used for a two-stage least squares estimation.
I define variables as follows:

\begin{itemize}

\item $P$: the log price of life, in dollars per QALY
\item $Q$: life expectancy in years
\item $I$: log GDP per capita, in dollars
\item $C$: a vector of control variables.
\item $\alpha_0$: the intercept term for the marginal benefit equation.
\item $\alpha_Q$: the coefficient for life expectancy in the marginal benefit equation.
\item $\alpha_I$: the coefficient for log GDP per capita in the marginal benefit equation.
\item $\alpha_C$: a vector of coefficients for control variables in the marginal benefit equation.
\item $\epsilon_{MB}$: the error term for the marginal benefit equation
\item $\beta_0$: the intercept for the marginal cost equation
\item $\beta_Q$: the coefficient for life expectancy in the marginal cost equation.
\item $\beta_C$: a vector of coefficients for control variables in the marginal cost equation.
\item $\epsilon_{MC}$: the error term for the marginal cost equation
\end{itemize}

The equation for the marginal benefit of life curve is as follows:

\[ P = \alpha_0 + \alpha_Q Q + \alpha_I I + \alpha_C \cdot C + \epsilon_{MB} \]

The equation for the marginal cost of life curve is as follows:

\[ P = \beta_0 + \beta_Q Q + \beta_C \cdot C + \epsilon_{MC} \]

I cannot use least squares to estimate $\beta_Q$ because $\epsilon_{MC}$ is correlated with $Q$.

However, if I solve for $Q$, I get an equation that I can estimate.

First, subtract the marginal cost equation from the marginal benefit equation:

\[ \alpha_0 - \beta_0 + (\alpha_Q - \beta_Q) Q + \alpha_I I + (\alpha_C - \beta_C) \cdot C + \epsilon_{MB} - \epsilon_{MC} = 0 \]

Now, solve for $Q$:

\[ Q = \frac{\alpha_0 - \beta_0}{\beta_Q - \alpha_Q} + \frac{\alpha_I}{\beta_Q - \alpha_Q} I + \frac{\alpha_C - \beta_C}{\beta_Q - \alpha_Q} \cdot C + \frac{\epsilon_{MB} - \epsilon_{MC}}{\beta_Q - \alpha_Q} \]

This is the equation I estimated in the first stage.

Then, 

\[ E(Q) = \frac{\alpha_0 - \beta_0}{\beta_Q - \alpha_Q} + \frac{\alpha_I}{\beta_Q - \alpha_Q} I + \frac{\alpha_C - \beta_C}{\beta_Q - \alpha_Q} \cdot C \]

So,

\[ Q = E(Q) - \frac{\epsilon_{MB} - \epsilon_{MC}}{\beta_Q - \alpha_Q} \]

Now, substituting this into the marginal cost equation:

\[ P = \beta_0 + \beta_Q \left(E(Q) - \frac{\epsilon_{MB} - \epsilon_{MC}}{\beta_Q - \alpha_Q} \right) + \beta_C \cdot C + \epsilon_{MC} \]
\[ = \beta_0 + \beta_Q E(Q) + \beta_C \cdot C + \beta_Q \epsilon_Q + \frac{\alpha_Q \epsilon_{MC} - \beta_Q \epsilon_{MB}}{\alpha_Q - \beta_Q} \]

This is the equation I estimated in the second stage.

\end{appendices}

